WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.777
After visually inspecting the data, start measuring it.

00:00:03.777 --> 00:00:08.177
There are several questions we can ask.

00:00:08.177 --> 00:00:13.487
For missingness, how many values are null, and how many contain only blank text?

00:00:13.487 --> 00:00:18.177
For validity, how many values violate a documented rule?

00:00:18.177 --> 00:00:23.052
For example, how many product prices are zero or negative?

00:00:23.052 --> 00:00:30.048
For uniqueness, how many possible duplicate groups appear after we normalize the fields used for matching?

00:00:30.534 --> 00:00:36.386
And for distribution, are there values that are unusual enough to deserve investigation?

00:00:36.386 --> 00:00:39.466
Imagine a dataset of patient ages.

00:00:39.466 --> 00:00:44.436
An age of ninety-five may be unusual but completely valid.

00:00:44.436 --> 00:00:48.836
An age of nine hundred fifty is almost certainly a problem.

00:00:48.836 --> 00:00:54.206
The important idea is that unusual does not automatically mean invalid.

00:00:54.206 --> 00:00:57.231
Profiling helps us find where to look.

00:00:57.231 --> 00:01:02.186
Business knowledge and documented rules help us decide what the values mean.
